{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS005254","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Among the 8 returned candidates, 4 are not direct matches to the target trait (they are hypertrophic cardiomyopathy models: PGS000739, PGS000778, PGS004910, PGS004911) and 2 are valve-function measurements rather than the disease endpoint (mean pressure gradient PGS005255; peak aortic velocity PGS005256). This leaves two direct aortic stenosis disease-endpoint models: PGS005252 and PGS005254, both in EUR development/evaluation and both using incident aortic stenosis phenotyping.

PGS005254 is preferred because it has clean/basic covariates (sex, age, age^2, PCs 1–5) and reports a per-SD HR with CI (HR=1.64, 95% CI 1.50–1.78), providing PRS-specific signal without being dominated by comorbidity adjustment. In contrast, PGS005252 reports a high full-model C-index (0.87) but includes multiple established cardiovascular comorbidities and risk factors (e.g., type 2 diabetes, hypertension, CAD, hyperlipidemia, BMI, smoking, renal failure), which the provided domain-knowledge flags as likely to inflate/disconnect full-model discrimination from PRS contribution; it also lacks PRS-only/incremental metrics.

Outcome is DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL (not DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY) because neither aortic-stenosis model provides clearly PRS-comparable discrimination metrics (PGS-only AUROC/R² or incremental AUROC), and PGS005254 lacks any reported AUC/R² entirely, limiting strength of comparative support."}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY",
  "best_model_id": "PGS000327",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Both candidates are concept-aligned to autism spectrum disorder by trait_reported/trait_efo, but PGS000327 has clearly better endpoint fidelity (phenotyping_reported: “Autism spectrum disorder”) and a disease-focused ASD GWAS publication context (“Identification of common genetic risk variants for autism spectrum disorder.”, Nat Genet). It also shows stronger reported validation performance evidence in the provided record (full_model_r2=0.0245 and OR per SD=1.33 [1.30–1.36]) with basic covariates (genetic PCs, genotyping wave) and larger validation sample (n=7,148) than PGS002790 (partial R²≈0.00546; phenotyping_reported: “Psychiatric behavior (withdrawn)” suggesting endpoint mismatch despite ASD labeling; validation n=2,198). Main limitation: both models lack clearly PRS-only metrics (e.g., PRS-only AUROC/R²), and the PGS000327 R² is labeled as full-model, so comparability is imperfect; hence confidence is moderate rather than high."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY",
  "best_model_id": "PGS000007",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Multiple direct-match breast carcinoma models are present. Among the clearly direct (trait_efo=breast carcinoma) candidates shown, PGS000007 (Breast cancer; LASSO penalized regression; 3,820 variants) has the strongest visible discrimination metric with full_model AUROC=0.78 (European validation) and is disease-focused (Am J Hum Genet breast-cancer PRS paper) with a reasonably sized validation sample (n=9,529). Competing direct-match models such as PGS000015 (P+T; AUROC=0.69; n=157,895) and PGS000332 (LDpred; C-index=0.75; n=37,841) show lower full-model discrimination in the provided metadata. Main limitation: for most candidates (including PGS000007), the context does not provide PRS-only AUROC/R2, and full-model AUROC may be covariate-influenced; additionally, some other direct-match models have much larger validation sizes, making the choice not unequivocal from the visible evidence alone."
}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS005271","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All three candidates are direct matches to the target trait: they share trait_efo = Hashimoto's thyroiditis and have the same phenotyping_reported (lymphocytic thyroiditis), indicating aligned endpoints. They also come from the same (disease-focused) multi-biobank publication context and have identical reported validation sample size (n=94,651) and evaluation ancestry (MAE; African American/Afro-Caribbean, Hispanic/Latin American, European), so evidence largely clusters rather than differentiates.

Among them, PGS005271 is preferred because, within this same endpoint/publication/validation context, it shows the strongest available PRS signal evidence via effect size (OR 1.549 vs 1.417 for PGS005272 and 1.037 for PGS005270) while also having a comparable full_model AUROC (0.6297; slightly below PGS005270’s 0.6387 but above PGS005272’s 0.6054). Method is only a weak tie-break, but PRSCS genome-wide shrinkage (PGS005271/PGS005272) is generally more plausible than a very sparse P+T score (PGS005270 with 55 variants) in the absence of other decisive evidence.

The selection remains sub-optimal because only full-model AUROC is reported (no explicit PRS-only AUROC/R2 and covariates are 'Unknown'), limiting comparability and making it hard to judge whether the small AUROC differences reflect PRS contribution versus covariate effects; thus confidence is not high despite clear direct-match availability."}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS005097",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Multiple direct-match candidates exist for heart failure. PGS005097 is preferred because its endpoint is directly aligned (phenotyping_reported: prevalent heart failure), it comes from an explicitly heart-failure-focused multi-cohort study context (many contributing biobanks/cohorts listed, suggesting externally oriented development rather than a pan-trait framework), and it reports reasonably informative discrimination (full_model AUROC 0.72 with basic covariates: age, sex, PCs) plus an additional metric (Brier skill score). However, confidence is limited because many candidates report only full-model AUROC/C-index (not PRS-only) and key comparability fields are missing or inconsistent across models (e.g., several have unknown covariates or lack PRS-only AUROC/R2), making it difficult to establish a clearly best standalone PRS contribution. Framework-style alternatives (e.g., PGS001790 global biobank PRS-CS-auto; PGS000709 UKB snpnet) and portability-style scores exist, but are down-ranked per provided policy when a disease-focused multi-cohort option with aligned endpoint is available."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS000639",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Direct-match Hodgkin’s lymphoma candidates are present (PGS000637–PGS000640; PGS003449). Among the PRSweb Hodgkin’s disease cluster (same publication, same EUR ancestry context, same covariates, same validation size n=775), PGS000639 (P+T, 20 variants) has the best reported discrimination (full_model AUROC=0.62) and strongest per-SD effect size (OR=1.476, 95% CI 1.154–1.889) versus the other PRSweb methods (AUROC 0.569–0.601; OR 1.292–1.377). PGS003449 is label-matched but has phenotype-field inconsistency (phenotyping_reported shown as chronic lymphocytic leukemia) and provides only a near-null OR (1.02, 0.97–1.08) with no AUROC/R2, reducing confidence. Overall, evidence is sub-optimal because the selected AUROC is a full-model metric (not explicitly PGS-only) and validation support for the Hodgkin’s models is small (n=775) with wide uncertainty for some secondary metrics (e.g., top 1% OR)."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS001043",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All 7 candidates are direct matches to the target disease concept (hyperthyroidism/thyrotoxicosis), but they split into (a) UK Biobank pan-trait/framework scores (PGS001042/PGS001043; snpnet across 813 traits; UKB-only) and (b) newer, more disease-focused multi-biobank Graves’ disease scores (PGS005264-266) plus UKB portability-framework thyrotoxicosis scores (PGS001815/PGS002023) with only partial-correlation metrics. For a generic hyperthyroidism target, PGS001043 has the clearest endpoint alignment ('Hyperthyroidism/thyrotoxicosis' in phenotyping_reported) and provides explicit PRS-only performance metrics (PGS-only AUROC=0.6323; PGS R2=0.0216) plus incremental AUROC (0.0464) under basic covariates (age, sex, array, PCs), making it the most directly comparable and interpretable option in the provided set. However, confidence is limited because the leading candidates (PGS001042 vs PGS001043) have near-identical performance and both come from a pan-trait UKB framework (a down-ranking context per domain_knowledge), while the multi-biobank Graves’ disease scores are more disease-study-focused but are subtype-specific (Graves’) and mainly report full-model AUROC with unknown covariates, reducing comparability. This leaves no single clearly high-quality winner from the visible evidence."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS004695",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Two direct-match candidates are present (PGS001791, PGS004695), both aligned to idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis by trait_reported/trait_efo. On endpoint fidelity, PGS001791 uses a generic disease endpoint (“Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis”), while PGS004695 is explicitly incident IPF (a narrower endpoint that domain guidance suggests modestly down-ranking for generic disease-risk deployment). However, PGS001791 comes from a pan-trait/global biobank framework-style publication (“Global Biobank analyses provide lessons…”) which domain guidance advises penalizing when a more disease-focused alternative exists. PGS004695 appears more disease-focused (IPF incidence in a prospective cohort; Chemosphere exposure-susceptibility framing) and reports a strong effect-size signal (HR 3.78 for high vs low PRS quintile) in a large European validation (n=402,042). The main limitation is non-comparability/absence of standard PRS discrimination metrics for PGS004695 (no AUROC/R2), while PGS001791 provides only a small PGS-only R2 (0.005929) and a full-model AUROC (0.758) that cannot be treated as PRS-only. Given these mixed and partially non-comparable evidence packages, PGS004695 is preferred but the selection remains sub-optimal with moderate confidence."
}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS003578","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Direct-match candidates for major depressive disorder are present (24 models). Several candidates have phenotype-aligned endpoints (e.g., PGS000138/PGS000145/PGS000193/PGS002759/PGS003333/PGS003576–PGS003585/PGS004591/PGS004759/PGS004760/PGS004885), but some are clearly endpoint-mismatched despite matching trait labels (e.g., PGS000767 validated on putamen volume; PGS000907 validated on fluoxetine muscle pain; PGS002789 validated on internalising behavior; PGS003442 validated on cognition/BOLD activation), which limits usable evidence.

PGS003578 is selected because it has high endpoint fidelity (phenotyping_reported: major depressive disorder; trait_efo: major depressive disorder), comes from a coherent, disease-focused study family with multiple closely related MDD scores (PGS003576–PGS003585) providing corroborating support, and reports one of the highest variance-explained metrics within that family (R²=0.0257) in a European validation (n=42,250) with only basic genetic-structure covariates (PCs 1–10). Competing models either (a) have weaker reported performance in comparable EUR validations (e.g., PGS000138 full-model AUROC 0.561; R² 0.018; PGS000145 R² 0.0032), (b) have performance reported in non-comparable ways or with unclear endpoint/metric interpretation (e.g., PGS000907 reports very high ‘Nagelkerke R2*100’ on a non-MDD endpoint; PGS004885 reports C-index for incident MDD but with very small training size and time-to-event framing), or (c) lack comparable R²/AUROC metrics (e.g., PGS002759 reports OR only).

Main limitation: most models (including PGS003578) do not report clearly PRS-only discrimination metrics (e.g., PGS-only AUROC), and reported R² values across studies may not be strictly comparable (often full-model or differently defined), so the choice is supported but not definitive."}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS000079",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Direct-match melanoma PRSs are present (multiple models with trait_efo=melanoma / phenotyping_reported melanoma). Among the visible candidates, PGS000079 is supported by strong endpoint fidelity (incident melanoma), large European validation (n=392,803), and reported discrimination/effect-size evidence (full-model AUROC 0.652; C-index 0.663; HR 1.43 [1.36–1.49]) with covariates that include standard demographics/PCs plus sun-exposure/skin-response variables (not biomarker-heavy). However, selection is not high-confidence because many other direct-match melanoma models are present in the context (103 total; only a subset shown), and several alternatives report higher AUROC but in much smaller or specialized cohorts (e.g., familial melanoma, small validation sizes) or with non-comparable reporting; additionally, most candidates do not provide clearly PRS-only metrics, limiting apples-to-apples comparison."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS002247",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Direct-match melanoma PRSs are present (e.g., PGS000079, PGS000118, PGS000732, PGS000743, PGS000766, PGS000813, PGS002246/PGS002247, PGS003430, etc.). PGS002247 is preferred because it has strong endpoint fidelity to the target trait (phenotyping_reported: incident invasive melanoma; trait_efo: melanoma), is from a disease-focused independent evaluation study, and reports comparatively strong discrimination among the listed melanoma models with comparable endpoints (full_model AUROC 0.691, R² 0.072, European evaluation; n=4,765). Limitation: many candidates report only full-model AUROC (often with varying covariate sets) and PRS-only metrics are generally absent, making cross-model comparability imperfect; additionally, PGS000743 reports a higher full-model AUROC (0.74) but on a much smaller validation sample (n=1,035) with extensive traditional risk-factor covariates, so the net evidence does not yield a single unambiguous high-confidence winner."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS000922",
  "confidence": "Low",
  "rationale": "All three listed candidates are direct matches at the disease-concept level (sarcoidosis), but the evidence does not support a clearly high-quality choice. PGS000922 and PGS000923 are from a pan-trait UK Biobank snpnet framework (“across 813 traits”), which domain guidance flags for down-ranking when better disease-focused alternatives exist; however, no disease-focused multi-cohort sarcoidosis PRS is present in this context. Between the two snpnet options, PGS000922 has the most endpoint-faithful phenotype for a generic sarcoidosis target (“Sarcoidosis” vs PGS000923’s time-to-event endpoint), while their reported European-validation discrimination is very similar (full-model AUROC ~0.649 vs ~0.655) with similar PGS-only AUROC (~0.643 vs ~0.646) and R² (~0.019 vs ~0.021). PGS001872 has a closer-to-sarcoidosis label but is for “skin sarcoidosis” in trait_efo and lacks AUROC/R² (only partial-r ~0.02 with a much smaller validation n=19,670), making it harder to compare directly. Given the reliance on UKB framework scores and the small, non-decisive metric differences plus phenotype/metric incomparability across candidates, confidence is low and the direct-match recommendation is sub-optimal."
}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS005220","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Direct-match candidates are present for sleep apnea, including multiple generic “Sleep apnea” scores (ExPRSweb family: PGS003204–PGS003218) and several “Obstructive sleep apnea” scores (PGS003479, PGS003857/PGS003858, PGS005219/PGS005220). Domain knowledge notes obstructive sleep apnea is the clinically dominant form of sleep apnea, so OSA-labeled models are endpoint-aligned for the target.

Among OSA candidates, PGS005220 (Obstructive sleep apnea, unadjusted BMI) comes from a disease-focused publication context (“Polygenic scores for obstructive sleep apnoea…”), uses a genome-wide PRS-CS-based approach, reports a materially higher full-model AUROC (0.79) than the ExPRSweb sleep-apnea framework scores (full-model AUROC ~0.49–0.53), and shows a strong per-SD effect size (OR=1.53, 95% CI 1.39–1.68). Covariates include only standard demographic/ancestry terms plus BMI; while BMI adjustment can affect comparability, the availability of an explicitly unadjusted-BMI version and strong effect size support makes this a reasonable direct-match pick.

Main limitations lowering this from high-quality: (1) for PGS005220 the catalog provides only a full-model AUROC (no PGS-only AUROC/R2 or incremental metrics), so PRS-specific discrimination cannot be isolated; (2) PGS005219 is very similar (same method and AUROC, stronger OR=1.98) but is BMI-adjusted, creating an unresolved choice between adjusted vs unadjusted formulations for a generic ‘sleep apnea’ target; and (3) some other OSA entries have metadata inconsistencies (e.g., PGS003479 phenotyping_reported ‘DBP’) or rely on very sparse GWS SNP sets (PGS003857/PGS003858), making cross-candidate comparison imperfect."}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS000611",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All 20 candidates are direct matches (trait_reported and/or phenotyping_reported are bladder cancer / cancer of bladder / urothelial carcinoma; all EUR-focused evaluation except two portability-style multi-ancestry evaluations). Several candidates report only full-model AUROC, which per provided domain_knowledge is not directly comparable to PRS-only performance, especially when covariates are included.\n\nAmong the candidates with PRS-comparable signals, the Cancer PRSweb UKB GWAS-hits score PGS000611 shows a coherent evidence package: endpoint matches (“Cancer of bladder”), clean/basic covariates (age, sex, batch, PCs 1–4), and relatively stronger reported discrimination within its study family (full_model_auc=0.567, pseudo-R²=0.0114, OR per SD=1.284, and a strong top-1% vs rest OR=2.66 with CI 1.79–3.93) on n=13,530 European evaluation, outperforming the closely related MGI-derived GWAS-hits version (PGS000610; AUROC 0.572 but smaller evaluation and less compelling top-1% OR with wide CI).\n\nHowever, confidence is limited because multiple other direct-match models have larger external validation samples and/or higher full-model AUROC (e.g., PGS000071/PGS000782 with ~0.80 AUROC but with substantial covariate inclusion and without PRS-only AUROC/R²), and several newer models do not report PRS-only metrics, making cross-candidate ranking under the provided comparability rules unresolved."
}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY","best_model_id":"PGS003381","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Direct-match candidates are present for the target trait uterine carcinoma, including one exact-label uterine-cancer model (PGS001795; phenotyping_reported: uterine cancer) and multiple clinically dominant subtype models for endometrial/uterine endometrial carcinoma (PGS000075, PGS000786, PGS002735-002737, PGS003381, PGS004244). Per provided domain guidance, endometrial carcinoma is the dominant subtype of uterine carcinoma and should be treated as a strong direct match; therefore endometrial/uterine endometrial carcinoma models are acceptable direct matches here.

Among these, PGS003381 has strong endpoint fidelity (phenotyping_reported: uterine endometrial carcinoma; trait_efo: endometrial carcinoma) and reports relatively favorable discrimination in its selected European validation (full_model_auc=0.761; R²=0.11) with only basic covariates (age, top 20 genetic PCs), making the performance evidence more interpretable than models whose reported AUROC may be more affected by richer epidemiologic covariates. It also avoids the “framework score” penalty applicable to PGS001795 (global biobank PRS-CS-auto framework context) and the sparse/older GW-significant-variant constructions that have similar or lower reported full-model AUCs.

Main limitation: metrics across candidates are not strictly comparable (many report only full-model AUROC with differing covariate sets, and several candidates have missing PRS-only metrics), so the preference for PGS003381 is based on the most coherent combination of dominant-subtype endpoint alignment plus comparatively strong, cleanly adjusted validation evidence rather than a definitive PRS-only performance comparison."}
